 120: ## T9 — Usage accounting, admission, processing tickets, and idempotency contracts
 121: Reasoning: Cost protection must hold under concurrency and replay before any provider call exists; this task 
owns what the database and ticket code can prove on their own (review P5). Dispatch-dependent evidence moves to T12.
 122: Task: Server-only usage records per company per day (America/Sao_Paulo). One `service_role`-only admission RPC 
that, in a single transaction holding the company settings row lock: (1) verifies the caller's current membership and 
editor rights on the note (for replays too); (2) looks up an existing operation for the idempotency key — if found 
with the same binding, returns its status without new admission, regardless of later settings changes; if found with a 
different binding, rejects; (3) otherwise verifies that the bound settings revision is current, reserves usage against 
the limit, records the operation with the provider/model/effort snapshot, and returns the matching key from Vault. 
Settlement once, against the admission day, by a `service_role`-only RPC; refunds only for pre-processing rejections. 
Idempotency keys scoped to user+company+operation and bound to note id, source revision, transcript identity, settings 
revision. Signed processing tickets (issue time, user, company, note, source revision, transcript identity, settings 
revision) with HMAC-SHA256 using the required server-only env var `MEETING_NOTES_TICKET_SECRET` (≥32 bytes, no 
fallback — AI start refuses with a configuration error when missing), plus optional 
`MEETING_NOTES_TICKET_SECRET_PREVIOUS` accepted for verification only so a rotation keeps outstanding tickets valid 
for their 24 h; the local value is provisioned in `.env.local` and `.env.example` documents it. A ticket-issue route 
(called when the notice is shown) authorizes current membership and editor rights before signing. Operation records 
without content kept 30 days (pg_cron cleanup); an operation without outcome 10 minutes after dispatch becomes 
"failed" and stays counted (pg_cron); deleting notes or users never changes usage; no client role can read-modify 
usage beyond reading its own company's count.
 123: Done when: Admission, settlement, replay lookup, and ticket issuance satisfy FR-022/FR-023/FR-031 at the 
database and route boundary, independently of any provider.
 124: Verify by: Integration tests: five concurrent admissions at 19/20 → exactly one reserved, four refused (AC-028); 
same key + binding returns the existing operation across midnight; different binding rejected; direct 
`anon`/`authenticated` calls to admission, settlement, and usage tables denied (AC-028); a settings save racing an 
admission yields either admission under the old revision with the old snapshot or refusal — never a mixed snapshot 
(AC-025 DB side); the stale-operation job turns an operation backdated >10 min into "failed", still counted; the 
cleanup job removes records older than 30 days; note deletion leaves usage unchanged (AC-034 DB side); limit-reached 
response data and reset at 00:00 America/Sao_Paulo (AC-016 DB side); ticket-issue route denies a removed member and a 
non-editor; `supabase-rls-reviewer` audit.
 125: Covers: FR-022, FR-023, FR-031, BR-008, AC-016, AC-025, AC-028, AC-034
